  "content": {
    "id": "p41-25j-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Alice tosses $n+1$ fair coins, while Bob tosses $n$. Find the probability Alice gets more heads than Bob.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac12$.\n\nBy symmetry, Alice's extra toss gives her half the tied outcomes.",
      "worked": "Let $A$ and $B$ count heads in Alice's first $n$ tosses and Bob's $n$ tosses. Let $H$ be 1 if Alice's extra toss is heads and 0 otherwise. By symmetry, $\\Pr(A>B)=\\Pr(B>A)$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(A>B)&=\\frac12\\bigl(1-\\Pr(A=B)\\bigr).\\end{align*}$$\nAlice wins when $A>B$, or when $A=B$ and her extra toss is heads. The extra toss is independent, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(A+H>B)&=\\Pr(A>B)+\\Pr(A=B,\\ H=1)\\\\\\Pr(A+H>B)&=\\Pr(A>B)+\\frac12\\Pr(A=B)\\\\\\Pr(A+H>B)&=\\frac12\\bigl(1-\\Pr(A=B)\\bigr)+\\frac12\\Pr(A=B)\\\\\\Pr(A+H>B)&=\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
